      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To perform open donor nephrectomy with the least amount of body alteration and complication, a short extraperitoneal incision was developed that preserves the ipsilateral rectus abdominis muscle and abdominal branches of the intercostal nerves."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Donor nephrectomy using the new incision was performed in 11 consecutive patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Morphine was required for pain relief for 1 to 3 days (mean 1.8), patients tolerated diet at 1 to 4 days (mean 2.2) and postoperative stay was 2 to 5 days (mean 3.5). Complications were minimal, consisting of postoperative fever 1 day in duration. A viable kidney was provided for transplantation in each case."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The extraperitoneal, rectus abdominis muscle and nerve sparing incision resulted in a short course of pain medication, early diet toleration and a short hospital stay."
        }
      ]},
